Literal spelling establishes a numeric origin. Moth keeps that spelling until
the permitted receiving and peer constraints have resolved its exact type.

### Spelling and defaults

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    whole = 42
    decimal = 0.5
    large = 1e6
    small = 1e-6
    precise = 1.0e+21
]

- Whole-number spelling creates an integer origin. An otherwise unconstrained origin defaults to `I32`.
- Decimal-point or exponent spelling creates a binary-float origin. An otherwise unconstrained origin defaults to `F64`.
- The grammar has no unsigned literal category, numeric suffixes or radix forms.
- Value size never chooses a width. An unconstrained whole literal outside `I32` diagnoses instead of retrying as `I64`, `U64` or `Dec`.
- A concrete receiver can materialise a retained whole spelling directly as `U64`, including values above the `I32` range.
- Decimal or exponent spelling cannot initialise an integer merely because its mathematical value is integral.
- Exponents use lowercase `e`. Uppercase `E`, unary `+` and a spaced prefix minus such as `- count` are invalid.
- A leading `-` stays attached to its literal or expression. Signed minimum values materialise directly from their negative spelling.

### Body-local constraints

Each runtime function body forms an inference boundary, including nested blocks,
branches, loops and handlers. The implicit root runtime body follows the same
rule. Each binding has one static type across all writes and control-flow paths.
Compile-time declarations resolve at their own declaration boundary, so later
runtime uses cannot retype a named constant. Each concrete generic instance
gets its own body-local inference state.

Value-producing blocks keep their existing placement and explicit-receiver
requirements. Inference operates inside legal constructs and does not invent a
missing receiver to legalise a different block form.

Numeric constraints can come from exact annotations, assignments, concrete
parameters, return slots, fields, typed container entries and eligible
value-producing receivers. Copies connect the same numeric unknown. Mutation
constrains one binding type rather than creating a new type for each write.
A known generic signature can connect an existing numeric unknown across its
parameters and result. The compiler uses that signature alone: it does not
inspect a callee body or search traits to choose a type.

Every eligible use contributes before defaulting. Source order cannot pick the
winning receiver. Conflicting exact receivers diagnose with both useful source
locations, independent of traversal order. A trait requirement, overload,
failure effect, proven value range, backend capability or optimisation never
chooses a numeric type.

This bounded process does not add general type inference. Function signatures,
fields, public interfaces and named constants remain concrete at their existing
declaration boundaries. No pending numeric unknown crosses a module boundary.

### Receiving, peer and default priority

Inference applies this priority before materialisation:

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    eligible receiving context
        -> eligible local numeric peer context
        -> language default
]

An exact receiver reaches unresolved literal arithmetic and grouping, including
raw literal leaves beside named operands. It never retypes a named binding,
constant, field, call result or completed operator result.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    base I32 = 10
    total I64 = base + 1
]

The receiver types raw `1` as `I64`, so the mixed fixed-integer operation
produces `I64`. `base` stays `I32`. If both operands name `I32` values, the
operation stays `I32` and an `I64` receiver rejects it.

Without an explicit receiver, an eligible typed numeric peer can supply its
exact type to the opposite unresolved literal-only arithmetic subtree. The
peer never changes its own type. A subtree containing a binding, field, call
result or cast result is not literal-only. Call arguments use their own
parameter receivers.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    small U8 = 10
    same_width = small + 1
]

The local `U8` peer types the raw `1` as `U8`, so `same_width` has type `U8`.
An explicit wider receiver can type the raw leaf wider without changing
`small`:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    wider U16 = small + 1
]

Here `1` receives `U16` and ordinary mixed-integer arithmetic produces `U16`.
`small` remains `U8`.

### Forward arithmetic only across named values

A later consumer can resolve an initializer that still consists only of
unresolved literal arithmetic:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    value = 1 + 2
    take_u64(value) -- Assume this parameter requires U64.
]

The initializer becomes `U64` before operator selection. A completed operation
over named values cannot search backwards for a wider operand type:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    base = 1
    twice = base + base
    take_u64(twice) -- Rejected without an independent U64 constraint on base.
]

`base` defaults to `I32`, so `twice` has type `I32`. The receiver cannot make
either named operand `U64`. An independent `take_u64(base)` can resolve `base`
before forward arithmetic. Its position relative to other eligible uses does
not change that result.

Copies preserve the same rule. A copied numeric unknown shares its type
constraints with the source, while a copy of a completed value keeps that
value's concrete type. Mutation also keeps one type across its initializer and
all later writes. A write cannot create a new type version or push a constraint
back through a completed named arithmetic expression.

Assume `take_u64` accepts a `U64` parameter:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    source = 1
    duplicate = copy source
    take_u64(duplicate)

    count ~= 1
    count += 1
    take_u64(count)
]

The copy connects `source` and `duplicate` to one numeric type. Mutation keeps
one type across `count`'s initializer, update and later use. Both examples
resolve to `U64` from the direct parameter receiver; source order does not
choose a different type.

### Literal families in collections

A non-empty numeric collection can retain one unresolved numeric element type
until an exact collection receiver or another eligible use resolves it. Raw
whole and decimal/exponent literals select the binary-float family regardless
of order:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    values = {1, 2.5}
    samples = {2.5, 1}
]

Both default to `F64` unless a receiver selects another binary-float precision.
A bound integer never changes family to fit:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    count = 1
    mixed = {count, 2.5} -- Rejected: count remains an integer.
]

An empty collection creates no numeric unknown and keeps its existing explicit
typing requirements. Arithmetic promotion does not search for a common
collection storage type.

### Destination-aware materialisation

A literal materialises directly at an immediate concrete numeric receiver.
The compiler does not first construct a default value and convert it:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    small U8 = 200
    large U64 = 18_000_000_000
    half F16 = 0.5
    money Dec2 = 12.34
    raw Byte = 255
]

- A whole literal can initialise a fixed integer, binary float, `Dec` or `Byte` when it fits.
- A negative value cannot initialise an unsigned type or `Byte`. Negative unsigned spellings, including `-0`, are rejected.
- Integer literals must fit the receiving range. `4_294_967_296` fits `U64` but not `U32`.
- Decimal or exponent literals must fit a receiving `Dec` scale exactly. `money Dec2 = 1.239` fails rather than rounding.
- A binary-float literal rounds directly to its destination using nearest, ties-to-even. Inexact values such as `0.1`, subnormals and signed zero remain valid, while a non-finite rounded result fails.

### `Dec` literal context

An explicit `Dec` receiver reaches every unresolved literal leaf in its
arithmetic and grouping. Each leaf materialises directly at the selected scale,
without passing through a binary float or a formatted intermediate:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    whole Dec4 = 1 + 1
    fraction Dec4 = 0.1 + 0.2
    grouped Dec4 = (1 + 2) * 3
]

`fraction` holds exactly `0.3`. Parentheses preserve the expression's grouping
but do not add a receiving boundary. A `Dec` peer can supply its scale to an
opposite literal-only arithmetic subtree from either side. A concrete `Dec`
value, call result or cast result does not become a raw literal.

Every decimal leaf must fit the selected scale. `small Dec2 = 0.005 + 0.005`
fails even though the mathematical sum fits because neither leaf can
materialise at scale two. Different `Dec` scales never rescale silently. A
completed operation over named operands also keeps its own type rather than
changing to satisfy a wider decimal receiver.

`Dec` context reaches a power base but not its exponent. The exponent uses the
separate `I32` rule below.

### Cast and operator barriers

A cast destination never chooses the source type. Resolve the source from its
own eligible constraints or its language default, then check the complete
source-target pair:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    value = 1
    take_u32(cast value)
    take_i64(value)
]

The later `I64` parameter resolves `value` as `I64`; the `U32` cast target does
not. The cast then uses the `I64`-to-`U32` policy. A cast also forms a barrier
to incoming arithmetic receiver context. An untyped declaration such as
`converted = cast source` remains invalid because no immediate target exists.

Already concrete operator results never change type to satisfy a wider
receiver. Comparison and logical results stop numeric context and remain
`Bool`. Member access, propagation and completed catch results also stop later
peer context. Explicit cast operands resolve independently.

`Dec` exponentiation has one separate boundary:

[table:
    [th: [: Base] [: Exponent type] [: Result]]
    [td: [: DecN] [: I32] [: DecN]]
]

An explicit `Dec` receiver reaches the power base but not the exponent. A raw
exponent receives the default `I32`; an already typed other-width integer needs
an explicit cast at a legal receiving site. Negative exponents keep their
existing failure behaviour.

### MON data boundary

MON literal data shares numeric spelling and materialisation facts but keeps
its independent schema-directed parser. Its integer and decimal/exponent
categories remain lossless until the schema selects a destination. See
@../mon/#mon-format (MON format).
